.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt INIT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm init
.Nd reap orphaned processes and maintain the console session and services
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm /sbin/init
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs as process 1.
It reaps orphaned children, continues stopped children, and respawns
the console session when it exits.
A pause between attempts prevents a failing login from being retried
continuously.
It never exits; the kernel panics if init dies.
.Pp
One source produces four builds, selected by the image and installed as
.Pa /sbin/init :
.Bl -tag -width init-recovery
.It Sy init-shell
Starts
.Pa /bin/sh
on the console in test images.
.It Sy init-login
Starts only
.Pa /bin/login .
No shell fallback is compiled into this build.
.It Sy init-metal
Starts the console login and supervises the system log, BusyBox's
.Ic syslogd
run as
.Ql syslogd -n -O /var/log/messages -s 200 -b 1 ,
which binds its socket where
.Pa /dev/log
leads and writes what
.Ic logger
and
.Xr syslog 3
send to
.Pa /var/log/messages ;
see
.Xr busybox-sysklogd 8 .
It supervises the SSH server:
OpenSSH's
.Xr sshd 8
where the image has
.Pa /usr/sbin/sshd ,
as the GNU system's root does, and
.Xr dropbear 8
on every other image.
Where the image has
.Pa /usr/bin/dbus-daemon
and the user
.Ql messagebus ,
as the GNU system's root does, it also supervises D-Bus's system bus,
started as
.Ql dbus-daemon --system --nofork --nopidfile --nosyslog
under that user's ids, with no supplementary groups.
Before each start it makes the bus's directory,
.Pa /var/run/dbus ,
owned by that user, and, when the file is missing,
.Pa /etc/machine-id ,
32 lowercase hexadecimal digits from the kernel's random numbers, which
the bus and its clients read as the machine's identity.
The bus is started as its own user because, started as root, it would
drop to that user with libcap-ng, which keeps a part of root's
capabilities across the change, and this kernel keeps all of a process's
capabilities or none; see
.Xr capget 2 .
An image with the program and no such user reports
.Ql init: dbus: no user messagebus
at boot and runs no bus.
Where the image has
.Pa /usr/sbin/crond ,
as the GNU system's root does, it supervises cronie's cron daemon, run as
.Ql crond -n ,
which logs to the system log and sends it what a job prints, since no
image has a mail transfer agent; see
.Xr cron 8 .
On a boot with
.Ql rach2.ip=none ,
it runs BusyBox's
.Ql ifup -a
to configure the network; see
.Xr ifup 1 .
.It Sy init-recovery
Provides the network and service functions of the metal build
and the recovery duties.
With
.Ql rach2.fsck
on the boot line, it first runs
.Ql e2fsck -p
on each of
.Pa /dev/disk0p1
through
.Pa /dev/disk0p4
whose superblock has the ext2 magic.
Its console session is a shell without a login, since the image has no
accounts of the machine's; see
.Xr recovery 7 .
.El
.Pp
The builds that start
.Pa /bin/login
on the console give it
.Ev LOGIN_TIMEOUT
set to 0, so the console's prompt waits without a limit; see
.Xr login 1 .
.Pp
Every build clears two levels of
.Pa /var/run
at boot, so that a directory such as the bus's goes with what is in it,
makes
.Pa /var/run/utmp ,
where glibc's programs keep the records of who is logged in, with only
the record of this boot, appends that record to
.Pa /var/log/wtmp ,
and reads
.Pa /etc/rach2/limits .
The boot record is sysvinit's: its type is BOOT_TIME, its user
.Ql reboot ,
its host the kernel's release and its time the
.Li btime
of
.Pa /proc/stat ;
coreutils'
.Xr who 1
.Fl b
and
.Xr uptime 1
take the boot time from it, and util-linux's
.Xr last 1
.Fl x
lists it.
It sets these resource limits on itself before starting any child;
see
.Xr limits 7 .
It starts
.Pa /bin/update
and keeps it running.
.Pp
Services receive
.Pa /dev/null
as standard input.
The service log is
.Pa /var/log/services .
When the log is available, init directs service standard output and
standard error there.
During operation, it empties the log in place when it has reached
256 KiB and writes:
.Dl init: the service log reached its limit and was emptied
.Pp
A service child exits before execution if a required descriptor cannot be
opened or duplicated.
Network setup runs in a child with the same redirection, leaving init's
console descriptors available for later login sessions.
The network worker and the SSH server's child reset SIGUSR1, SIGUSR2, and
SIGTERM to their default dispositions while they are blocked across fork,
then restore the inherited signal mask.
The bus's child does the same.
These signals terminate any of these children, the SSH server's while it
waits for
.Ql ssh-keygen -A
too, instead of shutting down the machine.
The console is reserved for the console session.
The kernel stops writing to the screen when init starts;
see
.Xr bootlog 4 .
Dropbear is started with
.Fl g
and
.Xr sshd 8
with
.Ql PermitRootLogin prohibit-password
unless
.Xr sshd.conf 5
permits root's password, when
.Fl g
is left out and
.Xr sshd 8
is given
.Ql PermitRootLogin yes .
.Xr sshd 8
runs in the foreground with
.Fl D
and writes its log to the service log with
.Fl e .
Before each of its starts,
.Ql ssh-keygen -A
makes the host keys missing from
.Pa /etc/ssh .
Each start of either server is reported on the console as
.Ql init: sshd: started as pid N .
.Pp
.Nm
handles the signals sent by the BusyBox shutdown applets:
.Bl -tag -width SIGUSR1
.It Dv SIGUSR1
Halt the machine.
.It Dv SIGUSR2
Power off the machine.
.It Dv SIGTERM
Restart the machine.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/rach2/sshd.conf
.It Pa /etc/rach2/limits
Initial resource limits.
.It Pa /etc/rach2/sshd.conf
SSH server policy.
.It Pa /etc/ssh
OpenSSH's configuration and host keys, on the GNU system's root.
.It Pa /usr/sbin/sshd
OpenSSH's server, which the metal build runs in place of Dropbear
when the image has it.
.It Pa /var/log/services
Service output and diagnostics, with the SSH server's record of each
login, readable only by root.
.It Pa /etc/machine-id
The machine's identity, made at the first boot of the GNU system's root.
.It Pa /usr/bin/dbus-daemon
D-Bus's system bus, which the metal build runs where the image has it.
.It Pa /usr/sbin/crond
The cron daemon, which the metal build runs where the image has it.
.It Pa /var/run
Entries cleared at boot, with the entries of its directories, which are
then removed; a directory deeper than that is reported if it is not empty.
.It Pa /var/run/dbus
The bus's directory, with its socket,
.Pa system_bus_socket .
.It Pa /var/run/utmp
The login records, the boot's first: OpenSSH's server on the GNU system's
root adds a session's record, which
.Xr who 1
reads; the musl programs keep none.
.It Pa /var/log/wtmp , Pa /var/log/btmp
The history of logins and of failed ones, which OpenSSH's server adds to
and util-linux's
.Xr last 1
and
.Xr lastb 1
read on the GNU system's root.
init makes each at boot when it is missing, the first with mode 0664 and
the second with 0600, and keeps what they have, adding the boot's record
to the first.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
The recovery file system check reports its status as:
.Dl init: e2fsck: /dev/disk0pN: status S
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr login 1 ,
.Xr bootlog 4 ,
.Xr sshd.conf 5 ,
.Xr limits 7 ,
.Xr recovery 7 ,
.Xr dropbear 8 ,
.Xr halt 8 ,
.Xr netup 8 ,
.Xr sshd 8 ,
.Xr update 8
